the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.75\n- Feedback: The network thoroughly assembles all literature-supported prebiotic routes to valine, capturing correct strategic bottlenecks (C5 skeleton formation), co-product formation, and environmental debates noted in the literature. The scheme is comprehensive and well-reasoned; however, almost all reactions (apart from the final reductive amination step) fail atom and/or charge conservation by the deterministic check. This reflects widespread formula/structure inconsistencies, missing balancing species (NH3, H2, CO2, H2O, O2, etc.), and incomplete or mis-specified intermediates. Hypothetical steps (pyruvate homocoupling/acetolactate pathway) are clearly flagged but are not experimentally demonstrated. The strongest, most defensible pathway is the \u03b1-KIV reductive amination (P4), provided \u03b1-KIV can be sourced; all other major routes must be revised for mass/charge balance and product mapping. To reach full approval, fix stoichiometry on all reactions (add missing small molecules), verify molecule IDs and specify experimental product yields and distributions as suggested.\n- Issues: ['The classic Strecker\u2013spark pathway is strongly literature-supported for the prebiotic detection of valine (Ring et al., Johnson et al.), co-forming with isovaline and norvaline. However, conservation violations in rxn_001 (unbalanced C, H, N) and both rxn_002 and rxn_003 (missing O, H, N adjustment products) are evident. Environment/conditions are appropriate, mechanism is valid, but steps must be mass-balanced and side-product fate clarified. Inclusion of isomer formation is positive. Fixing atom balance and specifying major byproducts would raise the score.', 'Cyanosulfidic acetone homologation (Patel et al., Sutherland et al.) is supported experimentally up through the valine \u03b1-aminonitrile, but rxn_004 is unbalanced (incomplete H, N, S), as are subsequent Strecker transformations. Mechanistically, the steps are defensible, but the lack of full atom conservation hurts feasibility. Environmental fit is appropriate (surface, UV, S), but must clarify S/N fates and yields. Folding in the correct byproducts and mass flows would substantially improve confidence.', 'Fischer\u2013Tropsch and discharge-style carbonyl synthesis connecting to Strecker is plausible in principle (Yoshino et al.), but rxn_005 and rxn_001 are highly unbalanced (severe element shifts). There remains debate about valine-specific yields. Environmental matching is very strong (hydrothermal / surface gas-phase). Mass conservation and selectivity must be carefully updated for robustness.', 'The pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV route is a hypothetical biosynthetic analog with no experimental prebiotic demonstration in the literature. rxn_007 and rxn_008 cannot be evaluated (missing molecules/IDs), and their chemistry lacks support (see literature review). Only the final amination is defensible. The hypothetical status of upstream steps undermines confidence, even if biochemically elegant.', 'HCN polymer hydrolysis produces valine as a trace product in some radiolysis conditions, but rxn_009 is grossly unbalanced (C, H, N, O), and mainstream HCN-pool hydrolysis does not yield valine. Pathway is at best marginal for target achievement and cannot be accepted as a principal route. Needs full mass balance, side product, and yield treatment.', 'Meteorite/interstellar ice photolysis is experimentally verified for trace valine formation but rxn_010 is unbalanced (missing C, N, H) and actual valine detected only after harsh hydrolysis. Nonetheless, this is a plausible environmental source for background valine, albeit at low concentrations. Must fix stoichiometry, clarify precursor-product relationship, and yield context.', 'The pantoic acid/pantolactone branch reflects modern side-paths (Fairchild et al. 2024, Miller and Schlesinger 1993) and competes with valine formation from isobutyraldehyde and formaldehyde. However, rxn_011 is unbalanced (N, H, O), and cyclization/reactivity details are missing. The pathway is important for selectivity modeling but needs full stoichiometric correction and byproduct accounting.', 'This side-pathway models co-formation of norvaline and isovaline as observed in Strecker/spark and meteoritic contexts. The major reactions (rxn_001, rxn_009, rxn_002) are unbalanced and have incomplete byproduct assignments (especially for isomer partitioning). The concept aligns with the literature, but the mass/charge errors and yield uncertainty for each isomer preclude full acceptance as currently written.', 'The cyanosulfidic\u2013Strecker convergence pathway (acetone \u2192 isobutyraldehyde \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 valine, with pantoic acid side-branch) reflects the multi-environment and feedstock-crossing nature of recent systems-chemistry literature (Patel et al., Sutherland group, Fairchild et al.). Core chemistry is plausible, but rxn_004, rxn_002, rxn_003, and rxn_011 are all unbalanced and require explicit side product and yield mapping. The environmental bridging is well-modeled, and selectivity caveats for syn-biosynthetic side products are present. Successful revision of mass balance and explicit product specification needed for approval.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network models all experimentally supported and literature-recommended prebiotic pathways to valine, including: (1) spark-discharge/Strecker synthesis via isobutyraldehyde (with competing side-branch to pantoic acid/pantolactone and co-production of norvaline, isovaline); (2) cyanosulfidic-acetone homologation route yielding valine aminonitrile followed by Strecker hydrolysis; (3) reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV) with various mineral/metal hydrogenation catalysts, as the end-game of the biosynthetic logic; (4) speculative non-enzymatic pyruvate homocoupling/acetolactate sequence; (5) HCN polymer/radiolysis/ice photolysis/meteoritic delivery as trace exogenous sources. All reactions are now fully atom- and charge-balanced with explicit small molecule byproducts and representative competing isomers/co-products. Side branches are explicitly depicted. Each pathway\u2019s chemistry is weighed for experimental support, environmental plausibility, and selectivity/covariate issues, drawing on all literature findings provided.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    { \"id\": \"mol_001\", \"formula\": \"CH4\", \"iupac_name\": \"methane\", \"common_name\": \"methane\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_002\", \"formula\": \"N2\", \"iupac_name\": \"dinitrogen\", \"common_name\": \"nitrogen\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/N2/c1-2\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_003\", \"formula\": \"H2O\", \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\", \"common_name\": \"water\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": null },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_004\", \"formula\": \"NH3\", \"iupac_name\": \"azane\", \"common_name\": \"ammonia\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": null },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_005\", \"formula\": \"H2\", \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\", \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\":