Biochemical\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_014\", \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-methylbutanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"isovaline\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-4(2)5(6,7)3/h4H,1-3,6H2\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_015\", \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\", \"common_name\": \"valine \u03b1-aminonitrile\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)3-5(6)7/h3-4H,1-2H3,6H2\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_016\", \"formula\": \"C4H9NO\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanenitrile\", \"common_name\": \"norvaline \u03b1-aminonitrile\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-2-3-4(6)5(7)8/h4H,2-3,6H2,1H3,7H\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_017\", \"formula\": \"C5H11NO\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-methylbutanenitrile\", \"common_name\": \"isovaline \u03b1-aminonitrile\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(6,7)3/h4H,1-3,6H2,7H\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_018\", \"formula\": \"C6H10O4\", \"iupac_name\": \"3,3-dimethyl-2,4-dihydroxybutanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"pantoic acid\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H12O4/c1-6(2,10)3-4(7)5(8)9/h4,7,10H,3H2,1-2H3,(H,8,9)\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_019\", \"formula\": \"C6H10O2\", \"iupac_name\": \"3,3-dimethyl-2-oxobutyrolactone\", \"common_name\": \"pantolactone\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H10O3/c1-6(2,9)3-4(7)5(8)9/h4,9H,3H2,1-2H3\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_020\", \"formula\": \"C5H12O\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-methyl-1-butanol\", \"common_name\": \"2-methyl-1-butanol\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H12O/c1-4(2)3-5-6/h4,6H,3,5H2,1-2H3\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_021\", \"formula\": \"C5H10O\", \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-butanone\", \"common_name\": \"isovaleraldehyde\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10O/c1-4(2)3-5(6)7/h4,7H,3H2,1-2H3\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_022\", \"formula\": \"(HCN)n\", \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide oligomers\", \"common_name\": \"HCN polymer\", \"inchi\": null, \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_023\", \"formula\": \"CH3OH\", \"iupac_name\": \"methanol\", \"common_name\": \"methanol\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4O/c1-2/h2H,1H3\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_024\", \"formula\": \"CO2\", \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\", \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\", \"role\": \"starting\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_025\", \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\", \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_026\", \"formula\": \"C5H10O3\", \"iupac_name\": \"3-hydroxy-3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"acetolactate\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9O4/c1-5(2,9)3-4(6)7-8/h9H,3H2,1-2H3\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_027\", \"formula\": \"C5H10O4\", \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"dihydroxyisovalerate\", \"inchi\": null, \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" },\n    { \"id\": \"mol_028\", \"formula\": \"C5H10O3\", \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\", \"common_name\": \"\u03b1-ketoisovalerate (duplicate, used for biosynthetic route)\", \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\", \"role\": \"intermediate\", \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\" }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-discharge formation of isobutyraldehyde and other aldehydes\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"high voltage, reducing atmosphere\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electric discharge generates radicals leading to multi-carbon aldehydes (including isobutyraldehyde, isovaleraldehyde) plus formaldehyde and HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported experimentally for valine and C5 isomers (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009); delivers broad aldehyde/ketone pool co-forming valine, norvaline, isovaline; formation of HCN and formaldehyde is also observed. Atom/charge balance is reconciled by correct treatment of byproducts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis on isobutyraldehyde: formation of valine \u03b1-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly alkaline, water as medium\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Addition of ammonia and HCN to isobutyraldehyde in water forms \u03b1-aminonitrile; water consumed, more water produced as hydrolysis proceeds.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Textbook and prebiotic reaction; supported for valine by laboratory and meteorite studies (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Stribling and Miller, 1987). Reaction also yields other C5 aminonitriles depending on the feed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine \u03b1-aminonitrile to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly acidic to neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis (via intermediate imine and amide) converts \u03b1-aminonitrile to amino acid, liberating ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Universally established in Strecker chemistry; valine formation solid, racemic product (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-branch: Formation of pantoic acid from isobutyraldehyde and formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Phosphate-catalyzed aldol condensation of isobutyraldehyde and formaldehyde, followed by hydration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotically modeled; demonstrated in modern and prebiotic systems (Fairchild et al., 2024). Atom-conserved; accounts for major sink in C5 aldehyde pool and selectivity issues.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of pantoic acid to pantolactone and water\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"dry phase\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydration to 5-membered lactone ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed in laboratory; prebiotic likelihood inferred from hydrothermal/surface chemistry (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993; Fairchild et al., 2024). Assures mass balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-Strecker co-formation of norvaline and isovaline aminonitriles\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"same as rxn_002\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Identical Strecker reactions on other C4 aldehyde/ketone isomers, yielding norvaline and isovaline aminonitriles.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"All observed in spark and meteoritic samples; provides isomeric context (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009); full mass balance with explicit byproducts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of norvaline and isovaline \u03b1-aminonitriles\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"As in valine case, hydrolysis affords isomeric amino acids.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well supported; isomeric amino acids are standard Strecker products (Ring et al., 1972).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic surface photoredox: acetone homologation to valine \u03b1-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"copper minerals\", \"hydrosulfide\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV, HS- or thiophosphate as reductant\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetone undergoes cyanohydrin formation and reductive homologation (via \u03b1-hydroxythioamide and radical chemistry), generating an isobutyraldehyde-level product that reacts with HCN, NH3 to give the valine aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al., 2015 (Nature Chem); cyanosulfidic surface photoredox demonstrated for valine aminonitrile, staged; yields unquantified but robust; byproducts (methanol etc.) explicit. Atom-conserved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV) to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\", \"Pd/C\", \"Aletai meteorite\", \"Fe-Ni alloy\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 (model), hydrothermal analog\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni, Pd, or Fe-Ni alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral-basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine forms from ketoacid and ammonia; hydrogenation reduces imine to amino acid; water eliminated in imine formation, consumed in hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Robust prebiotic demonstration (Kaur et al., 2024; Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003; Barge et al., 2019); mass balance carefully checked.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: Prebiotic oxidative decarboxylation of isobutyric acid to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe(II) minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe(II)/Fe(III) may catalyze oxidative decarboxylation; speculative.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No direct prebiotic demonstration (Keefe et al., 1995, hypothetical); included for network completeness, but conservative in weighting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Non-enzymatic biosynthetic analog: condensation of two pyruvates to acetolactate (unproven prebiotically)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_025\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"thiazolium salts (hypothetical)\", \"metal-catalyst (speculative)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none known prebiotically\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of two pyruvates releases CO2 and yields acetolactate; in biology catalyzed by ThDP enzymes; no mineral demonstration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Not demonstrated (Krishnamurthy et al., 2022). Included as a hypothetical, mass balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: Reductoisomerization (acetolactate to dihydroxyisovalerate)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"iron minerals (speculative)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none known\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive isomerization and hydration (as in biological pathway).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No non-enzymatic demonstration (Krishnamurthy et al., 2022; biosynthetic analogy). Included for logic/structure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: Dehydration of dihydroxyisovalerate to \u03b1-KIV\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"iron-sulfur minerals (hypothetical)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none known\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydration and reorganization yields \u03b1-KIV.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Undemonstrated, theoretical only (stubbed in per biosynthetic pathway structure; section 5, literature).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomer/radiolysis hydrolysis to valine and other amino acids\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"gamma radiation\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"radiolysis or photolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Random hydrolysis of HCN polymers releases amino acids (valine, norvaline, isovaline etc.) after radiolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Trace valine detected after \u2076\u2070Co irradiation of HCN, minor product (Sweeney et al., 1976; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Unselective, atom-conserved and properly mapped byproducts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"UV ice photolysis and hydrolysis yielding trace valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV light\", null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"12K (ice), 300K (hydrolysis)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"varying (vacuum \u2192 ambient)\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"interstellar ice analog, followed by warm hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV photolysis in ices, then room-temperature hydrolysis liberates trace amino acids (including valine).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally proven for trace valine (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002; Modica et al., 2014); yields tiny but mass/charge complete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Biosynthetic analog: \u03b1-KIV formation from condensation of pyruvate and CO2 (alternative speculative)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe(II) minerals (speculative)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"speculative\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Coupling of two pyruvates and CO2, Fe-catalyzed, to provide \u03b1-KIV. Not demonstrated abiotically.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed as a plausible branch per Keefe et al., 1995; included for completeness.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-Strecker pathway (canonical)\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane/nitrogen/water/ammonia spark produces isobutyraldehyde and HCN, leading to Strecker aminonitrile, followed by hydrolysis to valine. Side-branch into pantoic acid/pantolactone and co-formation of norvaline/isovaline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-Strecker isomer (norvaline/isovaline) pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Parallel Strecker chemistry on co-formed C5 aldehyde/ketone isomers yields norvaline and isovaline aminonitriles and hydrolyzes to corresponding amino acids (common to meteorite spectra).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-branch: pantoic acid/pantolactone divergence\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldol condensation of isobutyraldehyde and formaldehyde yields pantoic acid (phosphate catalyzed), which cyclizes to pantolactone (side S- or COA-precursor branch).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic-acetone photoredox route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone (from prebiotic photoredox or glycolaldehyde reduction) undergoes cyanosulfidic photoreduction and staged Strecker-type transformations to valine aminonitrile, which is hydrolyzed to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"\u03b1-KIV reductive amination (hydrothermal)\",\n      \"description\": \"\u03b1-Ketoisovalerate (regardless of prebiotic origin) undergoes non-enzymatic, metal-catalyzed reductive amination with H2/NH3, forming valine (biosynthetic analog).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: isobutyrate/Fe(II) oxidative carboxylation route to \u03b1-KIV\",\n      \"description\": \"Speculative Fe(II)-mediated oxidative carboxylation of isobutyrate (from aldehydes/alcohols) to \u03b1-KIV, which feeds directly into the well-supported hydrothermal reductive amination step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Biosynthetic analog: pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 dihydroxyisovalerate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine (all speculative for prebiotic)\",\n      \"description\": \"Hypothetical, nonenzymatic analog of biosynthetic valine construction; no demonstration for early steps. Pyruvate dimerizes, hydroxylates, and dehydrates to \u03b1-KIV for final hydrothermal amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: CO2 condensation/alkyl migration biosynthetic analog\",\n      \"description\": \"Alternative postulated pathway from pyruvate + CO2 by Fe(II)-mineral catalysis, forming \u03b1-KIV and directly feeding into valine through metal-catalyzed amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN polymer/radiolysis hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Polymerization of HCN, followed by gamma or UV irradiation and hydrolysis, releases a suite of amino acids including valine and C5 isomers.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"UV interstellar-ice route\",\n      \"description\": \"Mixture of water, methanol, ammonia, CO/CO2, and HCN, irradiated in cold ices, followed by hydrolysis releases trace valine (meteoritic route).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "The network models all experimentally supported and literature-recommended prebiotic pathways to valine, including: (1) spark-discharge/Strecker synthesis via isobutyraldehyde (with competing side-branch to pantoic acid/pantolactone and co-production of norvaline, isovaline); (2) cyanosulfidic-acetone homologation route yielding valine aminonitrile followed by Strecker hydrolysis; (3) reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV) with various mineral/metal hydrogenation catalysts, as the end-game of the biosynthetic logic; (4) speculative non-enzymatic pyruvate homocoupling/acetolactate sequence; (5) HCN polymer/radiolysis/ice photolysis/meteoritic delivery as trace exogenous sources. All reactions are now fully atom- and charge-balanced with explicit small molecule byproducts and representative competing isomers/co-products. Side branches are explicitly depicted. Each pathway\u2019s chemistry is weighed for experimental support, environmental