m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network models all experimentally supported and literature-recommended prebiotic pathways to valine, including: (1) spark-discharge/Strecker synthesis via isobutyraldehyde (with competing side-branch to pantoic acid/pantolactone and co-production of norvaline, isovaline); (2) cyanosulfidic-acetone homologation route yielding valine aminonitrile followed by Strecker hydrolysis; (3) reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV) with various mineral/metal hydrogenation catalysts, as the end-game of the biosynthetic logic; (4) speculative non-enzymatic pyruvate homocoupling/acetolactate sequence; (5) HCN polymer/radiolysis/ice photolysis/meteoritic delivery as trace exogenous sources. All reactions are now fully atom- and charge-balanced with explicit small molecule byproducts and representative competing isomers/co-products. Side branches are explicitly depicted. Each pathway\u2019s chemistry is weighed for experimental support, environmental plausibility, and selectivity/covariate issues, drawing on all literature findings provided.\n\nMOLECULES (28 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_002: N2 (nitrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: NH3 (ammonia) [star