 \"mol_014\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Electrical discharge, reducing gas mixture\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Gas-phase radical reaction induced by electrical discharge forms aldehydes (including isobutyraldehyde), ketones, ammonia and HCN from methane, nitrogen and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Atom-balanced as per Ring et al. (1972), Johnson et al. (2009): yields a suite of amino acid precursors (isobutyraldehyde, HCN, NH3, formaldehyde, ethanol, butanone, CO2). Literature supports co-formation of side-product isomers. See [1,2].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic aqueous solution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of isobutyraldehyde with ammonia and addition of HCN gives valine \u03b1-aminonitrile; water is both solvent and product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classic Strecker reaction as per Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013), Johnson et al. (2009). Atom balance maintained; liberation of one water by imine formation and addition. [3]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine \u03b1-aminonitrile to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hydrothermal or wet-dry cycle\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two-step hydrolysis of aminonitrile yields valine and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Widely verified (Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013, Strecker); atom conserving. [3]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic pathway: acetone homologation to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuS\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) on CuS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254-nm UV, cyanosulfidic cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetone converted to cyanohydrin, photoredox homologation with HCN and H2S/HS- under UV/copper produces isobutyraldehyde; water and HMT/H2S byproducts reuse intermediates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Balanced per Patel et al. (2015). Key intermediacy: isobutyraldehyde accessible by staged photoredox cycles; inclusion of HMT per cyanide phase. [Sutherland group, 2015]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-KIV to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\", \"Pd/C\", \"Aletai meteorite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar (H2 partial)\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or ground meteorite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH ~8, 72 h\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"\u03b1-Ketoacid undergoes imine formation with ammonia, hydrogenated by H2 over Ni catalyst to produce valine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fully atom balanced (Kaur et al., 2024; Barge et al. 2019). Only works with catalyst; yields 6\u201388%. [5,9,10]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative: Nonenzymatic biosynthetic analog pyruvate homocoupling to acetolactate (precursor to \u03b1-KIV)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Thiazolium salt absent; speculative\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two molecules of pyruvate condense (with hypothesized Fe2+ catalysis) releasing CO2 and formaldehyde to form \u03b1-KIV.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No nonenzymatic demonstration exists (see Muchowska et al. 2019; Keefe et al. 1995 hypothesis). Atom-conserving speculative analog to biology. Marked as speculative. [13,14]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of HCN polymer yields trace amino acids including valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acid hydrolysis after HCN oligomerization\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Complex polymerization of HCN followed by hydrolysis releases an ensemble of amino acids (mainly glycine/alanine; trace valine, norvaline, isovaline).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine confirmed only as minor product (Sweeney et al., 1976; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013). Atom balance for overall C-N chain as modeled in Ferris et al. (1974). [6,7]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs reaction on \u03b1-KIV\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Bicarbonate buffer, mild hydrothermal\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ketoacid plus ammonia, cyanide, and CO2 forms an aminonitrile hydantoin; hydrolysis yields valine plus two water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Atom balanced as per Pulletikurti et al. (2022); requires preformed \u03b1-KIV. [7]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Side reaction: pantoic acid branch from isobutyraldehyde and formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild, as per Fairchild et al., phosphate-catalyzed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol addition of isobutyraldehyde with formaldehyde followed by hydration yields pantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Atoms conserved. As per Miller and Schlesinger (1993), Fairchild et al. (2024); diverts carbon from valine path. [Fairchild et al. 2024]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonization to pantolactone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild wet-dry surface cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pantoic acid cyclizes, eliminating water, to form pantolactone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Atom balanced; follows known biochemistry and prebiotic experiments. [Fairchild et al. 2024]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-KIV yields \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate as major side product\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same as valine amination step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Competing hydrogenation at the carbonyl yields \u03b1-hydroxy acid; observed in all Ni\u2013metal-catalyzed aminations (Kaur et al. 2024).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Side-reaction with comparable yield to valine (Kaur et al. 2024); atom balanced. [5]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Fischer\u2013Tropsch\u2013type (FTT) surface formation of valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-Ni alloy\", \"Alumina/Magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"470-770K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni alloy, magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Dry, hot hydrothermal vent\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface Fischer\u2013Tropsch catalysis of CO, H2, and NH3 produces suite of amino acids including valine and its isomers, with CO2 as byproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Models Yoshino et al. (1971) FTT chemistry; valine among broad amino acid mixture; atom-balanced net. [8]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Interstellar ice photolysis yields amino acid precursors, hydrolysis yields valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV radiation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"12-150K (irradiation), then hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"low/high (ice/vapor phases)\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Simulated comet/asteroid analog; hydrolysis required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"VUV photolysis creates radical-based amino acid precursors, which convert to amino acids including valine after hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002); atom and environment balanced at net level. Minor valine yield, but experimentally verified. [9]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-Discharge Strecker Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane, N2 and H2O via spark discharge yield isobutyraldehyde, NH3 and HCN; Strecker synthesis then yields valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker from Exogenous Isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"Supply of isobutyraldehyde (from atmosphere or surface chemistry) enables a Strecker sequence without reliance on spark chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Acetone Homologation Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone homologated to isobutyraldehyde via photoredox cycles, feeding into the Strecker system.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"\u03b1-KIV Reductive Amination Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovaleric acid (\u03b1-KIV) with ammonia and H2 over Ni/meteorite catalysts yields valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative Biosynthetic Pyruvate Analog Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvic acid is condensed nonenzymatically (speculative) to \u03b1-KIV, which then undergoes reductive amination to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs Cyanide Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry (cyanide, ammonia, CO2, \u03b1-KIV) yields valine after hydantoin hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN Polymer Radiolysis/Hydrolysis Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN polymerizes under radiolysis; subsequent hydrolysis gives minor valine, norvaline, and isovaline yields.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Fischer\u2013Tropsch-Type Hydrothermal Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"CO, H2, and NH3 over Fe\u2013Ni or magnetite surfaces forms valine among a suite of amino acids.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_inter