m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This synthesis network for prebiotic valine integrates all experimentally demonstrated and literature-supported routes, explicitly correcting and tracking atom and charge balance for each reaction\u2014including competing sinks, co-isomer production, and speculative biosynthetic analogs. Ten converging pathways are constructed: (1) Spark-discharge Strecker synthesis from isobutyraldehyde, (2) direct Strecker formation using exogenous isobutyraldehyde, (3) cyanosulfidic homologation from acetone, (4) metal-catalyzed reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV), (5) Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry on \u03b1-KIV, (6) potential interstellar ice photolysis/hydrolysis, (7) radiolysis of HCN polymers, (8) Fischer\u2013Tropsch\u2013type hydrothermal synthesis, (9) competing pantoic acid branch, (10) a speculative biosynthetic analog from pyruvate. All pathways are atom-balanced, specify side-products, and precisely identify molecular structures and mechanistic detail. The C5 skeleton formation bottleneck is explicitly modeled, with speculative/hypothetical steps clearly marked.\n\nMOLECULES (28 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_002: N2 (nitrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (water) [starting