ement": 0.6, "overall_confidence": 0.41, "feedback": "The pantoic acid competitive branch models experimentally supported diversion of isobutyraldehyde to pantoic acid/pantolactone (per Miller/Schlesinger 1993, Fairchild et al. 2024). Both rxn_009 and rxn_010 are atom-unbalanced as written (N missing/excess; intermediates not matching formulas). Useful for network topology and competition modeling but chemically and stoichiometrically incomplete.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "suggestions": ["Balance all steps fully; clarify sources and sinks for formaldehyde, ammonia, etc.", "Align all intermediate formulae with their explicit structure."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P1", "overall_confidence": 0.72, "network_feedback": "This network captures all experimentally demonstrated and literature-supported routes to prebiotic valine, tracks key intermediates, and explicitly includes isomer/side-product generation and speculative skeleton-building branches. The topology aligns with best-practice recommendations in the literature, and environmental contexts are modeled with high fidelity. However, major chemical feasibility problems remain due to atom and charge conservation failures: only three reactions are balanceable with integer coefficients, and ten are unbalanced with clear C/H/N/O/S deficits. These errors reflect incorrect or incomplete specification of reactants and products in most reactions, undermining the validity of the network at the mechanistic level. Correcting these issues, especially for the key aminonitrile hydrolysis, cyanosulfidic homologation, and pantoate-branch steps, is necessary for network approval. The network's scope and design are strong, but chemical/stoichiometric rigor must be enforced. Overall, the network is not yet approved (overall_confidence < 0.9), but with targeted reaction balancing and formula corrections, it would provide an excellent, literature-aligned prebiotic valine synthesis model."}, "reason